      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-99055",
        "knowledge_point_id": "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions",
        "problem": "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5}?$",
        "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-5)-(x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-5)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)-(x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5+6x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+5}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+5}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=5.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=5.$",
