        "knowledge_point": "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities",
        "problem": "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        "worked_solution": "We use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
