        "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y={7}^{x+2}-3?$",
        "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- First, we plot the curve $y={7}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/4b2274f6e19ea02b6ebee1d9ac590c8186722be946f1d390ae4d27b2898d883e.png)\n\n- To plot $y={7}^{x+2},$ we shift the graph of $y={7}^{x}$ horizontally by $2$ units to the left. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-2,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/d900437ffd3ba0da1c6c25cbcff892e76929abe839fc8a49abea555c8163714f.png)\n\n- To plot the graph $y={7}^{x+2}-3,$ we shift the graph of $y={7}^{x+2}$ vertically by $3$ units down. The point $(-2,1)$ moves to $(-2,-2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/3c8f16780a5d9e3b655b9c7706fc664e9ef944d728d070c9cca886ac05b34504.png)",
